--TEST--
A call through a `callable` that names its parameters refuses a mismatched argument and a count the signature does not name, where they are written rather than one tag at a time at run time — `rule:types/callable-signature`
--FILE--
<?nvs
callable(int, string): string $join = fn (int $n, string $s): string => $s . $n;
// The argument is checked against the parameter the signature names. Through
// bare `callable` this is `nvs_runtime::callable`'s per-argument tag check and
// a `LogicError` at run time; here there is nothing left for it to decide.
echo $join("a", "b");
// The count is exact in this one direction: the value may hold a closure
// declaring fewer parameters than the type names, so a call passing fewer can
// leave one of *those* unfilled.
echo $join(1);
--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0401]: expected `int`, found `string`
  --> %s:6:12
  |
6 | echo $join("a", "b");
  |            ^^^ this is `string`

error[E0809]: this `callable` names 2 parameter(s), and this call passes 1
   --> %s:10:6
   |
10 | echo $join(1);
   |      ^^^^^^^^ expected 2, found 1
   = help: a call through a written signature passes exactly the parameters the type names. The value may be a callable that declares fewer, and it receives only the ones it declares

error: aborting due to 2 errors
To read what a code means and how to fix it, run `nvs agent show <code>`.
